1) Starting a program in a window
---------------------------------

Double-click !GraphTask in the Apps directory. Its icon (a little
window with a picture in it) appears on the right of the icon bar.

  Drag a BASIC file to the icon    the program runs in a new window.
                                   Drag several and each gets a
                                   window of its own.
  SELECT on the icon               a window at BASIC's > prompt, where
                                   you can type a program, RUN it,
                                   CHAIN "file", *Cat and so on
  MENU on the icon                 Info, New task (another > prompt
                                   window), Choices..., Quit

You can also drag BASIC files onto !GraphTask itself in a directory
display, whether or not it is running. Text files holding a BASIC
listing work too.

Other ways to get a window:

  - The Task Manager's menu (MENU on the Acorn icon) has "Graphics
    task window", just below "Task window": a window at the > prompt.
  - In !JsEdit, Run (Ctrl-R) on a BASIC listing saves it and runs it
    in a graphics task window, so you can see it beside the listing.
  - The commands in section 5.

Try the demonstrations in Demos.BASIC: drag Ceefax (a teletext page)
and BallPit (balls you throw with the mouse) to the icon, then some of
the others. Double-clicking them still runs them full screen.


2) Using the window
-------------------

The window starts the size of the program's screen: 640 x 512 for
MODE 12, 640 x 500 for MODE 7. When the program changes mode, the
window changes size to match, unless you have made it smaller
yourself. You can resize and scroll it like any other window.

  Keys     Click in the window to give it the input focus (its title
           bar turns cream). Keys, and INKEY with a negative number,
           reach only the program whose window has the focus. That
           first click only gives the focus: the program doesn't see
           it. F12 and the desktop's other keys still work.
  Mouse    MOUSE gives the pointer's position on the program's own
           screen, wherever the window is and however it is scrolled
           or scaled. SELECT and ADJUST go to the program; MENU opens
           the window's menu (see "Menu button" below).
  Escape   stops the program, as full screen. The window keeps its
           last picture and its title says "(finished)".
  Errors   appear in the window, as they would on the screen.
  Alt-Return   gives the program the whole screen; Alt-Return again
           brings it back into its window. It carries on running.
  Close    If the program is still running, GraphTask asks whether
           to kill it. A finished program's window just closes.

Programs keep running when their window doesn't have the focus, and
WAIT keeps a steady 50 frames a second.

To save the picture, drag it with SELECT while holding Shift to a
directory display: it is saved as a sprite called Screen. (Or use Save
screen on the menu.)

Dragging a file into a window whose program is running types the
file's name, as in a task window: handy at the > prompt.

Each window's program is listed in the Task Manager's Task display.


3) The window's menu
--------------------

Click MENU over a window:

  Menu button   MENU clicks go to the program (for programs that use
                all three buttons). Hold Shift and click MENU to get
                this menu back, and choose Menu button again to turn
                it off.
  Suspend       pause the program; Resume carries on
  Kill          stop the program; the window keeps the picture
  Restart       run the program again from the start (or a new >
                prompt)
  Speed         how fast BASIC runs: ARM2 (an A3000), ARM3 (an A5000),
                ARM610 (a Risc PC), StrongARM (a StrongARM Risc PC,
                the usual) or Unlimited (as fast as this computer can)
  Scale         1:1, x2 (twice the size) or Fit window (the whole
                screen fitted into the window, whatever its size)
  Full screen   the whole screen, as Alt-Return
  Save screen   a Save box: drag the sprite icon to a directory
                display to save the program's screen as a sprite file

Save screen saves the whole screen at its real size, whatever the
scale and however much of it the window shows. Suspend the program
first to catch the moment you want.


4) Choices
----------

Choose Choices... from the icon bar menu:

  Speed, Scale   what new windows start with
  Prompt windows start in MODE
                 the mode for a window at the > prompt (12 at first;
                 7 for teletext, 28 for 256 colours)
  Double-click runs BASIC in a window
                 when this is on, double-clicking a BASIC file runs it
                 in a window while GraphTask is loaded. When it is off
                 (as it starts), double-clicking runs programs full
                 screen, as RISC OS 3.71 does.

Set uses the choices until you quit; Save keeps them (in the file
Choices:GraphTask) for next time. Default fills in the usual ones.


5) Commands, Obey files and applications
----------------------------------------

These start !GraphTask if it isn't running:

  *GraphTask <file> [<arguments>]
          run the program in a window (the arguments are passed on,
          as with *BASIC -quit)
  *GraphTask
          a window at the > prompt
  *BASIC -window <file>
          the same as *GraphTask <file>
  Run <GraphTask$Dir> <file> <file> ...
          each file in a window of its own

They work in Obey files too. To make an application of your own that
opens in a graphics task window, give it a !Run file like this:

  | !Run file for !MyGame
  GraphTask <Obey$Dir>.!RunImage

and put the BASIC program in !MyGame.!RunImage. Double-clicking
!MyGame then runs it in a window.


6) Things to know
-----------------

  - A program that calls Wimp_Initialise becomes an ordinary desktop
    program and leaves its window.
  - Screen banks (*FX 112 and 113), sprites, OS_ReadModeVariable, POS,
    VPOS and MODE all belong to the window's own screen.
  - Quitting GraphTask (or shutting down) kills the programs in its
    windows; it asks first.
  - Some programs are still best full screen: double-click them, or
    use Full screen from the menu.
